<div class="tldr"><p><strong>Short answer:</strong> plan a ski trip backward from the snow. Pick your month from historical snowfall, fly into a hub airport with several resorts nearby, decide between a pass and day tickets, book flexible flights and lodging six to eight weeks out, and commit to a specific mountain about three days before you go, when the forecast is reliable.</p></div>

<h2>When to go</h2>
<p>Most North American resorts get their best snow from December through March, but every mountain has its own pattern. Coastal and northern resorts often load up early, while high Rockies resorts can stay deep into spring. Check the month-by-month snow picture for the resorts you're considering in the <%= link_to "free Powder Calendar", powder_calendar_path %> before you lock in dates.</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Early season (late Nov to mid-Dec):</strong> cheaper and quieter, but terrain can be limited until the base builds.</li>
  <li><strong>Holidays (late Dec, MLK weekend, Presidents' Day):</strong> the busiest and most expensive weeks. Book early.</li>
  <li><strong>Midwinter (Jan to Feb):</strong> usually the coldest, most reliable snow at most resorts.</li>
  <li><strong>Spring (March to April):</strong> longer days, softer snow, and often big storms at higher resorts.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Where to fly</h2>
<p>Instead of picking one resort and hoping it snows, fly into a hub airport with several mountains within driving distance. That way a storm anywhere in the region works for you. Denver and Salt Lake City are classic examples; see <%= link_to "ski resorts near Denver", hub_path("denver") %> and <%= link_to "ski resorts near Salt Lake City", hub_path("salt-lake-city") %>, or browse <%= link_to "ski resorts by state", regions_path %>.</p>
<p>Factor in the drive. A resort that looks close on a map can be hours away in a storm, especially over mountain passes.</p>

<h2>Choosing a resort</h2>
<p>Match the mountain to your group, not just the snow totals.</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Beginners:</strong> look for a dedicated learning area, a good ski school, and gentle, uncrowded green runs.</li>
  <li><strong>Mixed groups:</strong> a mountain with lots of blue terrain keeps everyone together.</li>
  <li><strong>Experts:</strong> steeps, trees, and a reputation for holding snow after storms.</li>
  <li><strong>Families:</strong> short walks to the lifts and easy logistics matter more than vertical. See the <%= link_to "family ski trip guide", guide_path("family-ski-trip") %>.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Pass or day tickets?</h2>
<p>If you're skiing only a few days at one resort, day tickets bought online in advance are usually the simplest choice; walk-up window prices are often the highest. If you'll ski several trips or multiple resorts, a multi-resort pass can pay off quickly. Compare which pass covers the mountains near your hub, and which is getting more snow right now, on the <%= link_to "Ikon vs Epic page", ikon_vs_epic_path %>.</p>

<h2>Where to stay</h2>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Ski-in/ski-out:</strong> you click into your skis at the door. The most convenient and usually the most expensive. Read <%= link_to "what ski-in/ski-out really means", guide_path("ski-in-ski-out") %> before booking.</li>
  <li><strong>Slopeside or walk-to-lift:</strong> a short walk to the base. A good balance of convenience and cost.</li>
  <li><strong>In town:</strong> often cheaper with more restaurants, but plan for a shuttle or drive and parking each morning.</li>
</ul>
<p>Book lodging with a flexible cancellation policy if you can, so you can shift resorts if the snow does.</p>

<h2>Getting there</h2>
<ul>
  <li>Rent a 4WD or AWD vehicle for mountain driving in winter.</li>
  <li>Check chain and traction rules for the roads you'll drive; some mountain highways require them during storms.</li>
  <li>Leave extra time on storm days. Roads to resorts can slow to a crawl or close temporarily.</li>
  <li>Check whether your lodging offers a shuttle; parking at some resorts is limited or paid.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Rentals and lessons</h2>
<p>Unless you ski often, renting is usually the better choice when you fly; see <%= link_to "rent or buy ski gear", guide_path("rent-or-buy-ski-gear") %>. Reserve rentals online before busy weeks, and book lessons early, since ski schools fill up on holidays. New skiers should start with <%= link_to "how to ski", guide_path("how-to-ski") %> and plan a lesson for day one.</p>

<h2>Budgeting your trip</h2>
<p>Costs vary widely by resort and week, so build your own estimate by category:</p>
<table>
  <caption>Ski trip budget categories</caption>
  <thead><tr><th scope="col">Category</th><th scope="col">What to account for</th></tr></thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><th scope="row">Lift access</th><td>Day tickets or pass, bought in advance</td></tr>
    <tr><th scope="row">Flights</th><td>Fares to the hub, plus checked bags if you bring gear</td></tr>
    <tr><th scope="row">Lodging</th><td>Nightly rate, resort fees, cleaning fees, and parking</td></tr>
    <tr><th scope="row">Ground transport</th><td>Rental car, gas, parking, or shuttles</td></tr>
    <tr><th scope="row">Rentals and lessons</th><td>Per day, per person</td></tr>
    <tr><th scope="row">Food</th><td>On-mountain lunches add up; groceries help</td></tr>
    <tr><th scope="row">Gear and extras</th><td>Anything you need to buy, plus après</td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>
<p>The <%= link_to "free Ski Trip Planner", ski_trip_planner_path %> includes a printable budget worksheet with these categories.</p>

<h2>Time it to the forecast</h2>
<p>Forecasts are most reliable within about three days and useful out to about seven. About a week out, check <%= link_to "where it's snowing this week", where_is_it_snowing_path %> to see which regions have storms lined up. Commit to a specific resort two or three days out, and plan to be in line for first chair the morning after a storm. More tactics in <%= link_to "how to chase powder", guide_path("how-to-chase-powder") %>.</p>

<h2>Common ski trip planning mistakes</h2>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Booking nonrefundable everything months ahead.</strong> You lose the ability to follow the snow or shift dates if a storm cycle misses your region.</li>
  <li><strong>Underestimating the drive.</strong> Mountain roads in a storm can double travel time. Arrive the day before your first ski day when you can.</li>
  <li><strong>Skipping altitude prep.</strong> At high resorts, drink plenty of water, go easy on alcohol the first night, and take it slow on day one.</li>
  <li><strong>Waiting to book lessons and rentals.</strong> Holiday weeks sell out, and morning rental lines eat into ski time.</li>
  <li><strong>Overpacking the schedule.</strong> Leave room for a rest day or a half day, especially on longer trips. Legs tire faster than you expect.</li>
  <li><strong>Ignoring the forecast until you arrive.</strong> A quick check a week out can tell you which mountain near your hub is about to get the most snow.</li>
</ul>

<h2>First trip checklist</h2>
<ol>
  <li>Pick your month and a hub airport.</li>
  <li>Decide on a pass or day tickets.</li>
  <li>Book flexible flights, lodging, and a 4WD rental.</li>
  <li>Reserve rentals and lessons.</li>
  <li>Pack with the <%= link_to "ski trip packing list", guide_path("ski-trip-packing-list") %> and check <%= link_to "what to wear skiing", guide_path("what-to-wear-skiing") %>.</li>
  <li>Watch the 7-day forecast and commit to a mountain three days out.</li>
</ol>
